#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""secure-linux-kit-update: use the local Secure Linux files, plus anything newer that was published.

One rule, used by the installer and by the USB creator:
  1. The local copy (on the drive, or already on this computer) is always enough on its own.
  2. With internet, compare the local release with the published one (one tiny file).
  3. Same release  -> nothing to do.
  4. Newer release -> fetch ONLY the files whose SHA-256 differs, verify every one, then swap them in.
  5. No internet, or any doubt at all (bad hash, older release, half-published state) -> keep the local copy.

Nothing is changed until every new file has been downloaded and has matched the published SHA-256.
This tool never fails an install: every refusal is reported and the local copy stays as it was.

Going back (undo): before `apply` swaps anything in, a copy of every file it replaces or removes is kept in
the folder .kit-update-undo inside the same Secure Linux folder (one earlier version only, size-limited).
`undo` puts that earlier version back exactly, checks every file by SHA-256, and can be run again if it is
cut short. An update that fails half-way, or is cut short by a power cut, is put back automatically.
A release that changed the release keys or the signature checker cannot be undone (it could trust a
withdrawn key again). After an undo this computer does not install that same release again until a newer
one is published, unless asked to (`apply --ignore-hold`).

Signed releases: once a release key is in the kit (share/secure-linux-release-keys), only a release whose
release.json and payload-files.json carry valid signatures is installed. The exact published file list and
both signatures are then kept here, so a copy made later without internet (the saved master) is checked
again. Only one update runs at a time on one folder (the lock file .kit-update.lock inside it).
online=no in /etc/secure-linux/kit-update.conf (or KIT_UPDATE_ONLINE=no in build.conf) means no network
request at all.

Safety check after every update (idea 12). Before anything is swapped in, every changed Python file is
compiled, every changed shell script is read with `bash -n`, a new copy of this tool must start, a changed
key folder must still load, and the update may not delete setup.sh, this tool, the signature checker or
revoked.txt. Any problem: nothing is installed. After the swap, setup.sh --help and this tool's --help must
work; if not, the update is undone at once (exactly as `undo` does) and that release is not installed again.
Either way one plain alert is written for the desktop (/var/lib/secure-linux/alerts.d/kit-update-check).
Run as root, setup.sh is only started when it stops at --help (setup.sh ignores words it does not know, so
a copy without that line would run a full setup).
Release history (C15): in signed mode the published history/releases.jsonl must carry a valid signature, end
with the published release, and continue the history this computer saw last (kept here); any doubt keeps
the local copy. A computer that never saw a history accepts the first one.
Where updates come from (C8 order): the published release on the internet (GitHub, https) first. In signed
mode only, then the home server copy (the mirror= folder of /etc/secure-linux/kit-update.conf, or
/media/homeserver/<HOME_SHARE_UPDATES>; only while that share is mounted and this computer is at home), then
other Secure Linux computers on this network (/run/secure-linux/kit-peers.json; plain http, because every
file is checked against the signed list). Every source passes exactly the same checks. When none of them
gives a newer, fully checked release, the local copy is used, as always.
Channel (idea 7): channel=testing in kit-update.conf reads the public testing branch; otherwise stable. In
signed mode the signed channel record (channel.json) must name this release; a stable computer takes only
releases whose record says stable. Once a record was seen here, a release without one is refused. The home
server copy and other computers are used only when they give a signed record for their release.
Downloads: four files at a time; a cut connection continues where it stopped (on this computer's own copy
also in the next run). The size is shown before a large download. --max-download-mb skips an update that
would download more than that when no person is at the screen (runs by itself).
A copy of the latest release with damaged or missing files (gap check HIGH 2): `check` says so, `apply` puts
back only those files, each checked against the list applied here.
Sets (ideas 21, 22): the Debian install files, the Flipper files and the network-install files are published
beside the payload. Their lists sit in payload-files.json under the optional key "sets" (signed with it; older
copies of this tool ignore the key). `check|apply --set debian|flipper|netboot --tree DIR` keeps a folder that
holds one set up to date with exactly the same checks; keys, settings and sources come from the Secure Linux files
this tool belongs to (the folder above its bin/, or /usr/local/lib/secure-linux for the copy in /usr/local/bin),
never from DIR. Only files this tool put in DIR (still unchanged) are ever removed.

  secure-linux-kit-update manifest --payload DIR --release-id ID --output FILE   (release tooling only)
  secure-linux-kit-update manifest ... --sets-from DIST     (adds the set lists of a rendered public tree)
  secure-linux-kit-update check --tree DIR
  secure-linux-kit-update apply --tree DIR [--ignore-hold] [--max-download-mb MB]
  secure-linux-kit-update check|apply --set debian|flipper|netboot --tree DIR [--max-download-mb MB]
  secure-linux-kit-update undo --tree DIR
  secure-linux-kit-update status --tree DIR [--json]                             (no network)
  secure-linux-kit-update activate --tree DIR        (helpers only: bring what setup installed up to date)
Exit codes: 0 done or safely kept; 1 undo refused; 2 usage; 75 another update is running.
"""
